<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edward Ullendorff</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1920">1920</a>–<a href="/years/2011">2011</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edward Ullendorff was a British scholar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semitic-languages">Semitic languages</a> and Ethiopian studies. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a> cite his judgement that Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zara-yaqob">Zara Yaqob</a> was the greatest Ethiopian ruler since the Aksumite era.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ullendorff was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> on 25 January 1920 to a secular Jewish family; his father, a wholesale merchant, died shortly before Edward turned 15. At the Graues Kloster grammar school he excelled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Greek, while teaching himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, serving as a cantor at the New Synagogue and attending lectures at the Hochschule für die Wissenschaft des Judentums. In September 1938 he fled the persecution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in Nazi Germany to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> with the help of the Youth Aliyah organisation, leaving his family behind.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At the Hebrew University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> he studied under the semitologist Hans Jakob Polotsky and in 1941 became its first graduate in Semitic philology. He then joined the British Military Administration in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, reading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amharic">Amharic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigrinya-language">Tigrinya</a> documents for the censorship, married Dina Noack at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asmara">Asmara</a> in 1943, and as assistant political secretary started the Eritrean Weekly News, the first Tigrinya-language newspaper. Back in Jerusalem after the war he processed compensation for victims of terrorist attacks for the mandate administration and was once kidnapped by the Irgun.</p><p>After 1948 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, completing an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> doctorate under G. R. Driver in 1951 on the relationship of the modern Ethiopian languages to Geʽez. He taught at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-andrews">St Andrews</a> from 1950 and was Professor of Semitic Languages and Literatures at Manchester from 1959 to 1964, when the School of Oriental and African Studies created for him the first chair of Ethiopian Studies anywhere. He added the SOAS chair of Semitic Languages in 1979 and retired in 1982; neither chair was filled after him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ullendorff was elected a Fellow of the British Academy in 1965 and gave its 1967 Schweich Lectures on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> awarded him the Haile Selassie International Prize for Ethiopian Studies in 1972, and Ullendorff translated and edited the emperor's autobiography. In a 1992 interview he said he had examined the ark kept at the Church of Our Lady Mary of Zion in Axum in 1941 and judged it a medieval construction. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> on 6 March 2011, and the British Academy founded the Edward Ullendorff Medal in his name in 2012.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Edward Ullendorff” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His own theological work, the Mashafa Berhan, set out doctrine for the Ethiopian church, and the scholar <b>Edward Ullendorff</b> judged him the greatest Ethiopian ruler since the Aksumite king Ezana.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kebra Nagast is divided into 117 chapters and is clearly a composite work; <b>Edward Ullendorff</b> describes its narrative as &quot;a gigantic conflation of legendary cycles&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/work/kebra-nagast">Kebra Nagast · What It Is</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.52,13.38&amp;year=1979" aria-label="Berlin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Edward Ullendorff"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5690 5568h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5690 5568h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.52,13.38&amp;year=1979">What was here in 1979? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
